{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfuiuqsPz3YGHYU6WCUuv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Vitest's `-t` (alias `--testNamePattern`) is a regex. It's matched against the test's full name, which is every enclosing `describe` name plus the test name, joined by spaces. For a test like `describe('math', () => { describe('add', () => { it('handles negatives', ...) }) })`, you could run:\n\n```sh\nnpx vitest run path/to/math.test.ts -t \"math add handles negatives\"\n```\n\nPutting the file path in the command keeps Vitest from searching other files, and quoting the pattern keeps the spaces intact. Because it's a regex, a shorter fragment like `-t \"handles negatives\"` also works, but it runs every test whose name contains it. To match only that one test, anchor the pattern (`-t \"^math add handles negatives$\"`) and escape regex characters like `(`, `)` or `.` in the name. For quick local runs, you can instead mark the test with `it.only(...)`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5810,"cache_read_input_tokens":15764,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5810},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"875789ea-803f-4ef5-943c-4f760082bcc3","uuid":"cd301204-6ee6-424d-a4a8-afd2ea4fe320","timestamp":"2026-10-11T03:16:00.592Z","request_id":"req_011CfuiuqZHj1hgE7i3r8KYR"}
